{"records":[{"id":"y85mnnzs9t","title":"LKB1 controls inflammatory potential through CRTC2-dependent histone acetylation","description":"Deregulated inflammation is a critical feature driving progression of tumors harboring mutations in the Liver kinase B1 (LKB1), yet the mechanisms linking LKB1 mutations to deregulated inflammation remain undefined. In this research publication we identify deregulated signaling by CREB regulated transcription coactivator 2 (CRTC2) as an epigenetic driver of inflammatory potential downstream of LKB1 loss. We demonstrate that LKB1 mutations sensitize both transformed and non-transformed cells to diverse inflammatory stimuli, promoting heightened cytokine and chemokine production. LKB1 loss triggers elevated CRTC2-CREB signaling downstream of the salt inducible kinases (SIKs), increasing inflammatory gene expression in LKB1-deficient cells. The immunoblot data contained in this dataset (for Figures 4, S2, and S3) show in murine LKB1-deficient MEFs and Kras/LKB1 (KPL) mutant lung cancer cells that CRTC2 is hypophosphorylated and CREB is hyperphosphorylated.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-04-24T18:53:35.911Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y85mnnzs9t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y85mnnzs9t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Russell","last_name":"Jones","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shelby","last_name":"Compton","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Inflammation","Cytokines","Cellular Signaling"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rm8kjxkkjv","title":"Original blots","description":"Original blots related to \"SUCLG1 Deficiency-Induced Histone Succinylation Impairs Oncogene Expression in Acute Myeloid Leukemia\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-24T16:04:21.305Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rm8kjxkkjv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rm8kjxkkjv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mengqing","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Acute Myelogenous Leukemia","Histone Modification"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hx4nwr8yyd","title":"Data for: Therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia developing 14 years after allogeneic stem cell transplantation, from a persistent R882H-DNMT3A mutated clone of patient origin.","description":"Supplementary (DOCX  2.34MB): \nMaterials and methods. \nFig. S1. TP53 mutation found in the lung carcinoma cells taken from the patient in 2012. \nFig. S2. Karyotype illustrating the metaphases of the 2015 therapy-related myelodysplastic syndrome cells. \nFig. S3. Paint of chromosome 15 (green) by fluorescent in situ hybridization. \nFig. S4. Analysis of the chimerism (Mentype® Chimera® software, Biotype) at 2015 therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis. \nFig. S5. IDH1, DNMT3A and NPM1 mutations found at the time of the acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis in 2001. \nFig. S6. DNMT3A and TP53 mutations found at the time of the therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis in 2015. \nFig. S7. Standard curve for the ASO-qPCR. \nFig. S8. Sensitivity assay for quantitative ASO-qPCR. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-18T12:28:12.179Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hx4nwr8yyd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hx4nwr8yyd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Iván","last_name":"Martín","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Felipe J","last_name":"Chaves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rosario","last_name":"Abellán","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Francisca","last_name":"García","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mar","last_name":"Tormo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlos","last_name":"Solano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan-Carlos","last_name":"Hernandez-Boluda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María Dolores","last_name":"Olivares","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eva","last_name":"Villamón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fernando","last_name":"Domingo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Blanca","last_name":"Navarro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marisa","last_name":"Calabuig","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paula","last_name":"Amat","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alicia","last_name":"Serrano","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cytogenetics"],"journal_issn":["00144800"]},{"id":"2gpjp3mbs6","title":"SETDB1 suppresses NK cell-mediated immunosurveillance in acute myeloid leukemia with granulo-monocytic differentiation","description":"The data include full immunoblot images for \"SETDB1 suppresses NK cell-mediated immunosurveillance in acute myeloid leukemia with granulo-monocytic differentiation\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-15T07:05:55.464Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2gpjp3mbs6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2gpjp3mbs6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yu-Hsuan","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hematology","Leukemia","Western Blot","Epigenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rdkg26mfjw","title":"CD37 a safe Chimeric Antigen Receptor target to treat acute myeloid leukemia","description":"CD37 a safe Chimeric Antigen Receptor target to treat acute myeloid leukemia - Raw data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-19T10:40:12.702Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rdkg26mfjw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rdkg26mfjw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sandy","last_name":"Joaquina","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"48s882jvnr","title":"Case report-CARE Checklist","description":"Care checklist for case report-Metachronous Presentation of BCL2-Positive Lung Adenocarcinoma and Acute Myeloid Leukemia: a case presentation and review of the literature","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-28T08:34:58.817Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/48s882jvnr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/48s882jvnr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bo","last_name":"Zheng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"56r624b4v2","title":"Defining the role of HIF-2alpha in the pathogenesis of acute myeloid leukemia and exploiting its inhibition as a new therapeutic approach to promote leukemia differentiation and exhaustion","description":"This dataset represents findings obtained within a project supported by the Italian Ministry of Health (project code: RF-2019-12369841) and focused on investigating the mechanism of myeloid differentiation blockade by the transcription factor HIF-2alpha in acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and the consequences of its inhibition with novel targeted therapies.\nWe found that in AML HIF-2alpha regulates a specific set of target genes involved in transcriptional repression, leading to inhibition of entire gene clusters linked to myeloid difefrentiation. As a consequence, HIF-2alpha blockade via genetic or pharmacologic approaches leads to myeloid differentiation and leukemia debulking. Also, we positioned HIF-2alpha under direct transcriptional control by the prodifferentiation agent all-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) and demonstrated that HIF-2alpha blockade cooperates with ATRA to trigger AML cell differentiation.\nLinks related to this dataset contain: 1. A publication describing part of the results funded by this grant; 2. Source data linked to this publication and deposited in the BioStudies repository (accession number: S-SCDT-10_15252-EMMM_202317810); 3. Sequencing data deposited in the GEO repository.","source":{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-11T06:49:50.887Z","url":"https://ordr.hsr.it/datasets/56r624b4v2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/56r624b4v2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosa","last_name":"Bernardi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pier Paolo","last_name":"Pandolfi","orcid":"0000-0002-5352-5295"},{"first_name":"Massimo","last_name":"Bernardi","orcid":"0000-0002-1334-0851"},{"first_name":"Daniela","last_name":"Magliulo","orcid":"0000-0003-3363-2736"},{"first_name":"Matilde","last_name":"Simoni","orcid":"0000-0002-5631-6763"},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Morelli","orcid":"0000-0003-1862-667X"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00789fa95","name":"Ministero della Salute","grant_id":"RF-2019-12369841"}],"categories":["Adult Myeloid Leukemia","Targeted Therapy","Acute Myeloid Leukemia Differentiation","Myeloid Differentiation","Hypoxia Signalling"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"89g4v8jgjf","title":"'Reduced intensity conditioning has the same overall survival as myeloablative conditioning for acute myeloid leukemia in complete remission and myelodysplastic syndrome—a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials","description":"The eight files are supplementary data of our systematic review 'Reduced intensity conditioning has the same overall survival as myeloablative\nconditioning for acute myeloid leukemia in complete remission and myelodysplastic syndrome—a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials' .","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-08T16:09:49.383Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/89g4v8jgjf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/89g4v8jgjf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yanzhi","last_name":"Song","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Supplementary Lighting"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6phjx8s467","title":"Data for: Enhanced antitumor effects of CD123/CD33 dual-antibody modified liposome targeting to acute myeloid leukemia cells with reduced the antigen-negative escape","description":"A dual-targeting drug delivery system was  developoed to enhance the therapeutic efficacy of chemotherapeutics and reduce the antigen-negative escape for Acute myeloid leukemia. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-07-18T23:35:51.324Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6phjx8s467","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6phjx8s467"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ShengJun","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Drug Delivery","Pharmaceutics","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":["03785173"]},{"id":"pbyjckj6g9","title":"SABRE HP Pyruvate Fluxomics in Mammalian Cells (U937 AML and Glial)","description":"Raw and processed data showing first results of SABRE hyperpolarized (HP) benchtop NMR fluxomics in U937 acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and glial cells, as well as general demonstrations of the technique details and technique flexibility.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-03T15:33:21.249Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pbyjckj6g9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pbyjckj6g9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Patrick","last_name":"TomHon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolism","Hyperpolarization","Hyperpolarized Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy","In Vitro Study","Fluxomics","13C NMR Spectroscopy"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":133085,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":162,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":133085,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132363,"restricted":69,"embargoed":653},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2250},{"id":"https://